
Topic 12: Static Synchronization ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This topic is actually easy because you already understand object synchronization.

The only new thing is:

Instead of locking an object, Java locks the entire class.

First, Let's Revise

=======================================================================================
Normal Synchronized Method

class Printer {

    public synchronized void print() {

    }

}

Question:

Where is the lock?

Answer:

On the object.

Example:

Printer p1 = new Printer();
Printer p2 = new Printer();

Visual:

Printer p1
   🔒 Lock 1

Printer p2
   🔒 Lock 2

So both objects can execute simultaneously.
=======================================================================================
Static Synchronized Method
class Printer {

    public static synchronized void print() {

    }

}

Notice the new keyword:

static

Now the lock is not on an object.

It is on the Printer.class object.
=======================================================================================
What is Printer.class?

Every Java class is loaded only once into memory.

Think of it like this:

Printer.class
        🔒

There is only one class lock, no matter how many objects you create.

Example
Printer p1 = new Printer();
Printer p2 = new Printer();
Printer p3 = new Printer();

Normal synchronized:

p1 → Lock 1

p2 → Lock 2

p3 → Lock 3

Static synchronized:

Printer.class

       🔒

Used by

p1

p2

p3

There is only one lock.

***Real-Life Example****

Imagine a college.

There are many classrooms.

Classroom A

Classroom B

Classroom C

Each classroom has its own door.

This is like object synchronization.

Now imagine the college has one main gate.

College Main Gate

Every student must pass through it.

This is like static synchronization.

No matter which classroom they belong to, everyone shares the same main gate.

=============================================================================

Memory Trick ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Object Synchronization

One Object
↓
One Lock


Static Synchronization

One Class
↓
One Lock

================================================================================

Real-Life Memory Trick

Imagine a hotel.

Object Synchronization
Room 101 🚪

Room 102 🚪

Room 103 🚪

Each room has its own key.

Different people can use different rooms at the same time.

Static Synchronization
Hotel Main Entrance 🚪

Only one person can pass through the entrance at a time.

Every guest must use the same entrance.

Interview Definition

A static synchronized method locks the Class object instead of an instance (object), 
ensuring that only one thread can execute any static synchronized method of that class at a time.


====================================================================================

| Feature                     | `sleep()`            | `wait()`                         |
| --------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Belongs to                  | `Thread`             | `Object`                         |
| Releases Lock?              | ❌ No                | ✅ Yes                           |
| Wakes Up                    | After specified time | After `notify()` / `notifyAll()` |
| Used Inside `synchronized`? | Can be used          | Must be used                     |
===================================================================================


⭐ One Important Rule

This is asked frequently in interviews.

Can we call wait() anywhere?

❌ No.

You must call it inside a synchronized method or synchronized block.

Example:

synchronized(this) {
    wait();
}

If you write:

wait();

outside synchronization, Java throws:

java.lang.IllegalMonitorStateException

Reason: The current thread doesn't own the object's lock, 
so it isn't allowed to call wait().

Why?

Remember our bathroom analogy.

To "leave the bathroom and release the lock" (wait()), you must already be inside the bathroom (inside the synchronized block).

If you're standing outside, you cannot release a lock that you never acquired.

That's why Java enforces this rule.
====================================================================================

Next Topic: wait() and notify() (Thread Communication)

This is one of the most important topics in Java because it's the first time threads communicate with each other.

Until now:

sleep() → Thread waits by itself.
join() → One thread waits for another thread to finish.
synchronized → Threads compete for a lock.

Now you'll learn something new:

One thread tells another thread: "You can continue now."

This is called inter-thread communication.
===================================================================================

One Important Difference

Many beginners think:

wait()
=
sleep()

❌ Wrong.

sleep()
↓
Wake up after Time

wait()
↓
Wake up after Notification

=================================================================================

| `sleep()`                 | `wait()`                               |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Time-based                | Notification-based                     |
| Does not release the lock | Releases the lock                      |
| `Thread` class            | `Object` class                         |
| Resumes automatically     | Resumes after `notify()`/`notifyAll()` |
=================================================================================

The Word You Should Remember

Interviewers often use this word:

Polling

sleep()

↓

Polling

↓

Repeated Checking

Whereas:

wait()

↓

Event Driven

↓

Wait for Notification
⭐ Memory Trick

sleep()
↓
Alarm Clock
"I'll wake up after 5 minutes."


wait()
↓
Phone Call
"Call me when you're done."

This is probably the easiest way to remember the difference.

======================================================
Memory Trick ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Remember this sentence:

notify() wakes the thread, but it does not give it the lock immediately.

The waiting thread must wait again until the notifying thread releases the lock.
*******Notice the wording:Get ready, not start immediately.**************
